    "result_text": "There's only one test class, and it passed. The full build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the structured audit event in `ClinicServiceImpl.saveOwner(...)`, alongside the existing human-readable audit line.\n\n**Changes in `src/main/java/.../service/ClinicServiceImpl.java`:**\n\n1. **Structured event emission** \u2014 on owner creation, after the existing audit line, the service now emits an immutable JSON event via the `AUDIT` logger:\n   ```json\n   {\"seq\":1,\"ownerId\":11,\"customerCode\":\"NSW-16E6609E\",\"membershipLevel\":1,\"event\":\"OWNER_CREATED\"}\n   ```\n\n2. **Monotonic `seq`** \u2014 a process-wide `static final AtomicLong OWNER_CREATED_SEQ`, incremented once per create, guarantees a monotonically increasing sequence across all creates.\n\n3. **Future-proof identifier sourcing** \u2014 the identifier value comes from a single `primaryIdentifier(Owner)` helper that returns `owner.getCustomerCode()` today. The spec calls out that the customerCode will later be unified into a `memberId`; when that happens, changing that one helper makes the event carry the `memberId` automatically, with no other edits. I documented this intent in Javadoc on both `ownerCreatedEvent` and `primaryIdentifier`.\n\n4. **JSON serialization** uses Jackson 3 (`tools.jackson`, already the project's mapper), with a `LinkedHashMap` so field order matches the spec `{seq, ownerId, customerCode, membershipLevel, event}`.\n\nThe `AcceptanceTest` (verifying an `AUDIT` line contains `OWNER_CREATED`, the owner id, and the customerCode) passes, and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures).\n\nNote: this is not a git repository, so I've made no commit \u2014 let me know if you'd like anything else.",
